<p>Living in Hawaii means trading mainland prices for ocean views, and the trade isn't cheap. Housing, groceries, gas and utilities all cost more than almost anywhere else in the country. That's why so many residents run side hustles in Hawaii — and why the smart ones lean into what makes the islands different: millions of visitors a year, a strong military presence, a rich craft and food culture, and a time zone that leaves your afternoons free if you work for mainland clients.</p>
