<h2 id="get-tax">Hawaii's GET license: the rule every side hustler needs</h2>
<p>This is the part most newcomers miss. Hawaii doesn't have a typical sales tax — it has a <strong>general excise tax (GET)</strong> on gross receipts from almost any business activity, including services, freelancing, crafts and gig work.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You need a license.</strong> Anyone receiving business income in Hawaii must register with the Department of Taxation for a GET license. Apply online at Hawaii Tax Online with Form BB-1; the fee is a one-time $20.</li>
  <li><strong>The rate.</strong> The state rate is 4%, and every county adds a 0.5% surcharge, so most side hustlers owe 4.5% of gross receipts.</li>
  <li><strong>It's on gross, not profit.</strong> GET is calculated on what you take in before expenses. Price it in — many businesses show a GET pass-on on invoices.</li>
  <li><strong>File regularly.</strong> You file periodic returns (monthly, quarterly or semiannually depending on how much you owe) plus an annual reconciliation.</li>
  <li><strong>Rentals have extra taxes.</strong> Rentals of less than 180 days also owe transient accommodations tax (the state rate rose to 11% in 2026) plus a county TAT.</li>
  <li><strong>Income tax still applies.</strong> Hawaii income tax rates run from 1.4% up to 11%, on top of federal income tax and 15.3% self-employment tax.</li>
</ul>
